// Encyclopedia Diagrams + Deep Dives pass -- user request: "Go back through
// the Football Encyclopedia and make sure every possible thing that could
// have a diagram ... gets one ... also if u can't use a diagram add a deep
// dive." Investigated directly: data/football-diagrams.js has 55 real
// diagrams (14 formations, 10 fronts, 10 coverages, 11 pass concepts, 10 run
// concepts), and app.js's existing f101DiagramFor() already links a concept
// to a diagram whenever their canonical_id matches EXACTLY (39 of the 682
// Encyclopedia concepts already got this for free). This file covers the
// real remaining gap across the 7 domains the user explicitly named
// (Coverages, Run Game, Blocking, Pass Protection, Route Tree, Formations,
// Defensive Fronts): concepts that are real, named VARIANTS of an existing
// diagram (e.g. Cover 3 Cloud vs. the base Cover 3 diagram) get a
// `diagramOverride` pointing at the closest real base diagram, shown with an
// honest "closest related diagram" caveat (see f101DiagramFor()'s isVariant
// handling in app.js) rather than pretending it's an exact match. Every
// entry gets a `text` deep dive -- real, standard football teaching content,
// not sourced to a specific workbook (sheet, row) the way the base
// Encyclopedia's own SOURCE_BACKED fields are, so this is deliberately kept
// in its own file rather than merged into data/learn-encyclopedia.js's own
// DB-generated content. Organizational "how X concepts are grouped" nodes
// (OTAX_*/DTAX_* canonical_ids) are intentionally excluded -- they're
// taxonomy headers, not real teachable football concepts.
window.ENCYCLOPEDIA_DEEP_DIVES = {
  // ---- COVERAGES ----
  "COVER_2_MAN": { diagramOverride: "COVER_2", text: "Cover 2 Man (also called \"2-Man\" or \"Man Under, Two Deep\") pairs man coverage on every eligible receiver underneath with two deep safeties splitting the field, unlike zone Cover 2's underneath zone drops. It gives tight, physical coverage while still keeping a safety over the top on each half -- a common red-zone and third-down call because it takes away quick option routes without fully exposing the deep field." },
  "COVER_1_ROBBER": { diagramOverride: "COVER_1", text: "Cover 1 Robber is base Cover 1 (man coverage, one deep free safety) with a twist: instead of purely deep, the free safety \"robs\" underneath, reading the quarterback's eyes to jump a route in the middle of the field -- most often used to bait a quarterback into a crossing route or dig that the robber can undercut." },
  "COVER_2_INVERT": { diagramOverride: "COVER_2", text: "Cover 2 Invert flips the usual roles: a safety rotates down to play an underneath zone while a cornerback or slot defender bails to a deep half, disguising the coverage post-snap. It's used to confuse a quarterback's pre-snap read that expects standard Cover 2 leverage." },
  "COVER_3_CLOUD": { diagramOverride: "COVER_3", text: "Cover 3 Cloud rolls the corner down to the flat (a \"cloud\" rotation) instead of a deep third, with the safety rotating over to cover that deep third instead -- closing the classic Cover 3 vulnerability to the flat/curl combination on that side, at the cost of needing the safety to cover more ground." },
  "COVER_3_SKY": { diagramOverride: "COVER_3", text: "Cover 3 Sky keeps the corner deep in his third (unlike Cloud) and instead rotates a safety down into the flat/curl area, usually to the side away from the strength call, giving extra run support or flat help while a deep third stays intact." },
  "COVER_3_BUZZ": { diagramOverride: "COVER_3", text: "Cover 3 Buzz has a safety \"buzz\" down into an underneath hook/curl zone (rather than the flat, as in Sky) while a cornerback and the other safety hold the two remaining deep thirds -- a common way to get an extra body in the middle of the field against crossing routes." },
  "SINGLE_HIGH_SHELL": { diagramOverride: "COVER_3", text: "A single-high shell describes the pre-snap picture of one deep safety in the middle of the field -- the shared alignment behind Cover 1, Cover 3, and their variants. Because several different coverages share this exact look before the snap, offenses must diagnose the real coverage from post-snap rotation, not the shell alone." },
  "TWO_HIGH_SHELL": { diagramOverride: "COVER_2", text: "A two-high shell shows two safeties split evenly, each roughly a hash width from the middle, before the snap -- the shared pre-snap look behind Cover 2, Cover 4, and Cover 6. Like a single-high shell, the specific coverage underneath is only confirmed once the defense reacts to the snap." },
  "PRESS_TECHNIQUE": { text: "Press technique lines a defender up at the line of scrimmage, directly on a receiver, to jam him at the snap and disrupt his release before he can build speed into his route. It buys extra time for the pass rush and denies a free release, but leaves a corner vulnerable to a quick, well-executed release move if he doesn't get his hands on the receiver cleanly." },
  "OFF_TECHNIQUE": { text: "Off technique (also called \"soft\" technique) has a defender line up several yards off the receiver's line of scrimmage rather than jamming him at the snap, giving up a free release in exchange for keeping the receiver in front and reacting to his route rather than his get-off." },
  "TRAIL_TECHNIQUE": { text: "Trail technique has a defender line up slightly behind a receiver, deliberately conceding leverage on one side to trail the route rather than mirror it -- most often used against a receiver expected to run an inside-breaking route, betting the defender can rally underneath it." },
  "BAIL_TECHNIQUE": { text: "Bail technique has a defender start in a press alignment at the snap, then immediately open up and retreat (bail) into a deep zone drop -- a disguise technique meant to look like man press coverage before the snap while the defense is actually rotating into a zone shell." },
  "CATCH_TECHNIQUE": { text: "Catch technique is a patient man-coverage style where the defender mirrors the receiver's release and stays square, waiting to \"catch\" and react to the route's break rather than jumping it aggressively -- trading some risk of a big completion for far fewer costly penalties or blown coverages from guessing wrong." },
  "LEVERAGE": { text: "Leverage describes which side of a receiver a defender aligns to before the snap -- inside or outside -- which tells you which route the defense expects help on. Outside leverage invites an inside route (with safety help expected over the top or inside), while inside leverage invites an outside/fade route." },
  "CUSHION": { text: "Cushion is the pre-snap distance a defensive back gives a receiver before the snap. A big cushion buys time to read the route but concedes an easy short completion; a small cushion (press-like, without necessarily jamming) takes away the short game but is more exposed to a quick release beating it deep." },
  "MEG_TECHNIQUE": { text: "MEG (Man Everywhere he Goes) is a coaching rule that tells a defender to stay in man coverage on his assigned receiver no matter where that receiver goes on the field -- even through what looks like a zone-triggering route combination -- as opposed to a \"banjo\" or pattern-match rule that could hand him off to a teammate." },
  "ROBBER_COVERAGE": { diagramOverride: "COVER_1", text: "Robber coverage generally describes any defender -- often a safety -- dropping into an underneath zone specifically to read the quarterback's eyes and jump an intermediate route, rather than dropping to a fixed area regardless of the quarterback's read. Cover 1 Robber is the most common named version, but the same robbing principle can be layered onto other coverages too." },
  "BRACKET_COVERAGE": { text: "Bracket coverage puts two defenders on one especially dangerous receiver -- one underneath, one over the top (or one inside, one outside) -- surrendering a defender elsewhere on the field in exchange for taking that one receiver almost entirely out of the game." },
  "PALMS_COVERAGE": { diagramOverride: "COVER_4", text: "Palms (also called \"2-Read\") is a Cover 4 pattern-match rule for the corner and flat defender on a 2-receiver side: if the outside receiver runs a quick out or comeback, the corner drives on it and the safety rallies to take over the vertical threat from the inside receiver; if the outside receiver runs vertical, the corner carries him deep instead." },
  "BANJO_COVERAGE": { text: "Banjo (or \"switch\") coverage is a man-coverage rule for two defenders covering two receivers who are close together (like a bunch or stack): if the receivers cross or exchange, the two defenders simply pass off (\"banjo\") their assignments to each other rather than fighting through traffic to stay on their original man." },
  "PREVENT_DEFENSE": { text: "Prevent defense drops extra defenders deep to guard against a long touchdown, almost always used late in a half or game with a lead, conceding short and intermediate completions in exchange for taking away the deep shot that could tie or win the game in one play -- famously criticized for \"preventing you from winning\" when it lets an opponent methodically march down the field underneath it." },
  "COVERAGE_DISGUISE": { text: "Coverage disguise is the practice of showing a defensive back and linebacker alignment before the snap that suggests one coverage, then rotating into a different one immediately at or after the snap -- meant to get the quarterback's pre-snap read wrong so he throws into leverage he didn't expect." },
  "COVERAGE_POACH": { text: "A poach is when a defender assigned to a zone (or a different man) opportunistically jumps a route he reads developing nearby, \"poaching\" a throw that wasn't technically his primary responsibility -- a coached, situational aggressiveness rather than a blown assignment, usually reserved for very predictable route combinations." },
  "COVERAGETECH_LATE_ROTATION": { text: "A late rotation is a disguise technique where the secondary holds a static pre-snap shell as long as legally possible, then rotates into its real coverage at the last instant before or right at the snap, minimizing how long the quarterback has to correctly diagnose it." },
  "COVERAGETECH_SHOW_PRESSURE_BAIL": { text: "Show pressure, bail is a disguise where the defense lines up as if it's about to blitz -- extra defenders crowding the line of scrimmage -- then drops those same defenders back into coverage at the snap instead, hoping the quarterback's pre-snap protection call and route concept were built to beat a blitz that never comes." },

  // ---- RUN_GAME ----
  "RUN_PIN_PULL_SWEEP": { diagramOverride: "RUN_SWEEP", text: "Pin-and-pull (or \"pin-pull\") sweep has playside linemen \"pin\" (block down on) whoever lines up over them while backside linemen \"pull\" around to lead the runner outside -- a zone-blocking-influenced way to run sweep without needing a fullback or lead blocker, common in modern zone-run offenses." },
  "RUN_SPLIT_ZONE": { diagramOverride: "RUN_INSIDE_ZONE", text: "Split zone runs the same inside- or outside-zone blocking scheme up front, but adds a tight end or H-back running across the formation opposite the play call to kick out (or log) the backside edge defender -- creating a cutback lane while still reading zone blocking rules." },
  "RUN_MID_ZONE": { diagramOverride: "RUN_INSIDE_ZONE", text: "Mid zone (sometimes called \"tight zone\") aims the running back's initial track a bit wider than inside zone but tighter than wide/outside zone, giving him a true one-cut read between a cutback and a bounce depending on how the defense flows." },
  "RUN_OUTSIDE_ZONE_BOOT": { diagramOverride: "RUN_WIDE_ZONE", text: "Outside zone boot pairs the outside (wide) zone run action with a quarterback bootleg pass off the same look -- the offensive line and running back sell the zone run while the quarterback fakes the handoff and rolls out to throw off it, attacking a defense that's overplaying the run." },
  "RUN_RPO": { text: "An RPO (run-pass option) is a single play call that gives the quarterback a real handoff and a real pass read built into the same snap: he hands the ball off unless a specific defender (his \"read\" key, often a linebacker or safety) reacts a certain way, in which case he pulls it and throws instead -- forcing that one defender to be wrong no matter what he does." },
  "RUN_POWER_READ": { diagramOverride: "RUN_POWER", text: "Power read (or \"power option\") runs power blocking up front but leaves the backside edge defender unblocked as an option read for the quarterback, the same way a zone read leaves a defender unblocked off zone blocking -- if he crashes down on the running back, the quarterback keeps it around him." },
  "RUN_TRIPLE_OPTION": { diagramOverride: "RUN_ZONE_READ", text: "The triple option gives the quarterback three real possibilities off one snap: hand off to a dive back, keep it himself, or pitch to a trailing back, each decision made by reading an unblocked defender in sequence (first the dive read, then the pitch read) rather than blocking every defender." },
  "RUN_WHAM": { diagramOverride: "RUN_TRAP", text: "Wham is a trap-blocking variant where a fullback, tight end, or H-back (rather than a pulling lineman) comes across the formation to kick out an unblocked defensive lineman, letting the rest of the offensive line block as if it were a normal zone or gap play." },
  "RUN_INSIDE_VEER": { diagramOverride: "RUN_ZONE_READ", text: "Inside veer is a triple-option play that reads the first defender inside the tackle (usually a defensive end) for the dive-or-keep decision, then reads a second, wider defender for the keep-or-pitch decision -- a staple of option offenses like the Flexbone and Wishbone." },
  "RUN_MIDLINE": { diagramOverride: "RUN_ZONE_READ", text: "Midline is an option run that reads a defensive tackle or nose guard (rather than the edge defender, as in a standard zone read) immediately after the snap, handing the ball to a quick dive back if that interior defender doesn't tackle him, or keeping it if he does." },
  "RUN_SPEED_OPTION": { diagramOverride: "RUN_ZONE_READ", text: "Speed option has the quarterback attack the edge immediately at full speed with a trailing pitch back, reading the first unblocked defender outside the tackle -- if that defender commits to the quarterback, he pitches; if the defender sits to defend the pitch, the quarterback keeps it upfield." },
  "RUNTECH_PRESS_THE_LANDMARK": { text: "Pressing the landmark means a running back drives hard and full speed toward his assigned aiming point before making any cutback decision, rather than drifting or hesitating laterally -- pressing the defense's front to force it to fully commit before the back reads where the real crease is." },
  "RUNTECH_TEMPO": { text: "Tempo, in the run game, refers to the pace and rhythm of the running back's footwork through his mesh with the quarterback and into the line -- a back who is late or early relative to his blockers' timing can turn an otherwise well-blocked run into a loss, even with the right rushing lane blocked." },
  "RPO_ACCESS_THROW": { text: "An access throw is the pass option built into an RPO -- usually a quick, easy throw like a bubble screen or slant that's already \"accessible\" the instant the read defender vacates his area, requiring almost no extra time for the quarterback to throw accurately once he's made the run/pass decision." },
  "RPO_CONFLICT_READ": { text: "A conflict read is the specific defender an RPO is designed to put in an unwinnable spot -- he has a real run responsibility and a real pass responsibility in the same area, and the offense's play design guarantees he can't fully cover both no matter which one he reacts to." },

  // ---- BLOCKING ----
  "BLOCKTECH_BASE_DRIVE_BLOCK": { text: "A base (or drive) block is a lineman blocking the defender lined up directly across from him, driving him straight back off the line of scrimmage -- the most fundamental block in football, and the assignment on nearly every gap-scheme run play for the linemen not pulling or double-teaming." },
  "BLOCKTECH_REACH_BLOCK": { diagramOverride: "RUN_WIDE_ZONE", text: "A reach block asks an offensive lineman to get his body across (or \"reach\") a defender aligned to his outside shoulder, turning the defender's shoulders away from the play -- the signature block of outside/wide zone runs, since the whole scheme depends on linemen winning the race to the defender's outside number." },
  "BLOCKTECH_DOWN_BLOCK": { diagramOverride: "RUN_POWER", text: "A down block angles inward against a defender lined up to the blocker's inside, rather than blocking straight ahead -- the standard assignment for playside linemen on gap-scheme runs like Power or Counter, sealing the inside gap so a pulling blocker has a lane to kick out or lead through." },
  "BLOCKTECH_COMBO_TO_LINEBACKER": { diagramOverride: "RUN_INSIDE_ZONE", text: "A combo block has two offensive linemen double-team a defensive lineman at the snap, then one of them climbs off to block a linebacker at the second level once the double-team has controlled the down lineman -- the defining technique of zone-blocking schemes." },
  "BLOCKTECH_KICK_OUT": { diagramOverride: "RUN_POWER", text: "A kick-out block attacks a defender from the inside out, driving him wider and away from the formation's inside gaps -- the classic job of a pulling guard or lead fullback on Power, opening the exact gap the running back is aimed at." },
  "BLOCKTECH_WRAP_LEAD": { diagramOverride: "RUN_COUNTER_GT", text: "Wrap (or lead) blocking sends a puller around and through the hole ahead of the running back to block the first threatening defender he encounters at the second level, rather than a pre-determined man -- common on Counter and Power plays where a guard pulls all the way through the line." },
  "BLOCKTECH_ARC_RELEASE": { diagramOverride: "RUN_ZONE_READ", text: "An arc release has a tight end or H-back release outside, arcing around the edge defender rather than blocking him directly, to get to a linebacker or safety at the second level -- often used on zone-read and option plays where the edge defender himself is left unblocked as the read." },
  "BLOCKTECH_SIFT_SPLIT_FLOW": { diagramOverride: "RUN_COUNTER_GT", text: "A sift (or split-flow) block sends a fullback or H-back across the formation opposite the direction the offensive line is flowing, to block a linebacker who's flowing with the line -- used on counter and misdirection runs to seal a defender who'd otherwise run right to the ball." },
  "TECH_STANCE_AND_WEIGHT_DISTRIBUTION": { text: "An offensive lineman's stance and weight distribution -- how his weight is balanced between his hands and feet, and how square or angled his shoulders are -- determines what blocks he can execute explosively without tipping off the defense which direction or type of block is coming." },
  "TECH_FIRST_STEP_ECONOMY": { text: "First-step economy means a blocker's first step covers the minimum ground necessary to get on track to his target, rather than a long, slow lunge -- a short, quick, purposeful first step keeps a lineman's base and balance intact and gets him to contact faster than the defender he's blocking can react." },
  "TECH_HAT_PLACEMENT": { text: "Hat placement is where a blocker's helmet ends up relative to the defender at the moment of contact -- getting your \"hat\" on the correct side of a defender (inside or outside, depending on the assignment) determines which way he can be driven and is one of the first things a lineman's technique is graded on." },
  "TECH_INDEPENDENT_HANDS": { text: "Independent hands means a blocker's two hands can strike, grip, and control separately from each other rather than punching as one unit -- letting him control a defender with one hand while using the other to fight off a swipe, club, or counter move." },
  "TECH_ANCHOR": { text: "Anchor is a blocker's ability to stop his own backward momentum and hold his ground against a bull rush, using leverage and lower-body strength rather than upper-body strength alone -- losing your anchor is what a collapsed pocket around the quarterback usually looks like." },
  "TECH_HALF_MAN_RELATIONSHIP": { text: "Half-man relationship describes a blocker positioning his body so his head and near shoulder are aligned with the midpoint of the defender he's blocking -- a positioning rule that keeps him able to react to either side the defender tries to go, rather than committing too early to one shoulder." },
  "TECH_REACH_BLOCK": { diagramOverride: "RUN_WIDE_ZONE", text: "The reach-block technique itself is a lateral first step that gets a blocker's near foot ahead of the defender's outside foot before he ever tries to gain ground upfield -- winning the race with the feet, then finishing with the hands, rather than trying to out-muscle a defender straight ahead." },
  "TECH_CUTOFF_BLOCK": { diagramOverride: "RUN_INSIDE_ZONE", text: "A cutoff block, on backside zone runs, asks a lineman to get his body between a backside defender and the play, cutting him off from pursuit even if he can't drive him off the ball -- the goal is only to seal the running lane, not to win a physical battle." },
  "TECH_DOWN_BLOCK": { diagramOverride: "RUN_POWER", text: "Down-block technique emphasizes a short inside step and getting the head across the defender's inside number immediately, since a down block gives up outside leverage on purpose -- any hesitation lets the defender squeeze back inside before the blocker's angle is set." },
  "TECH_KICK_OUT_BLOCK": { diagramOverride: "RUN_POWER", text: "Kick-out technique attacks the outside number of the target defender with the near shoulder, aiming to turn his shoulders and hips away from the formation -- a kick-out blocker who instead gets driven backward in place still lets the ball carrier get strung out too wide." },
  "TECH_LOG_BLOCK": { diagramOverride: "RUN_COUNTER_GT", text: "A log block is the mirror image of a kick-out: instead of turning the defender outward, the blocker seals him inside, \"logging\" him toward the formation's interior so the ball carrier can bounce all the way outside the block -- used when the defender being blocked has already gained outside leverage." },
  "TECH_WRAP_PULL": { diagramOverride: "RUN_COUNTER_GT", text: "Wrap-pull technique is the footwork of a pulling lineman tracing a tight, flat path along the line of scrimmage before turning up through the hole -- pulling too far behind the line (\"bowing out\") costs the runner the extra half-second he needs the blocker to arrive on time." },
  "TECH_SKIP_PULL": { diagramOverride: "RUN_COUNTER_GT", text: "A skip pull is a short-distance pulling technique -- a lineman pulling only one gap over uses a quick lateral skip step rather than a full open-and-run pull, getting to his target almost as fast as a normal down block would." },
  "TECH_COMBO_TO_LINEBACKER": { diagramOverride: "RUN_INSIDE_ZONE", text: "The technique of climbing off a combo block to a linebacker is timed off the down lineman's own movement, not a fixed count -- climbing too early lets the down lineman fight loose from the double-team, while climbing too late lets the linebacker run free to the ball." },
  "TECH_SCOOP_COMBINATION": { diagramOverride: "RUN_WIDE_ZONE", text: "A scoop (or scoop combination) is a double-team where both blockers work in the same direction across the formation, rather than one driving straight and one climbing -- common on outside/wide zone, where the whole line is moving laterally together toward the play side." },
  "TECH_PASS_SET_ANGLE": { text: "Pass-set angle is the direction a lineman's initial kick-slide step takes him relative to the rusher -- a tackle protecting the edge sets at an angle toward the sideline to cut off a speed rush around the corner, while a guard protecting inside gaps sets on a much flatter, more vertical angle." },
  "TECH_POST_FOOT": { text: "Post foot is whichever foot a pass blocker plants and pushes off of to redirect against a rusher's counter move -- keeping that foot underneath his own body (rather than overextended) is what lets a lineman recover mid-rep instead of getting knocked off balance the moment a rusher changes direction." },
  "TECH_SET_LINE_INTEGRITY": { text: "Set line integrity means every pass-protecting lineman sets to a consistent, matching depth off the ball, rather than some setting deeper than others -- a broken set line creates a gap in the pocket wall that a rusher can run through untouched even if every individual matchup nearby is being won." },
  "TECH_PASSING_OFF_A_TWIST": { text: "Passing off a twist (or stunt) is the communication between two adjacent blockers when the defensive linemen in front of them cross -- the blocker whose man is looping around must alert his teammate to pick up the looping rusher while he takes over the man crossing into his own gap." },
  "TECH_SHORT_SET_PROTECTION": { text: "A short set has a pass blocker take a much smaller initial kick-slide than a normal vertical set, used on quick-game passes where the ball is coming out fast enough that the blocker only needs to buy a half-second, not stonewall a full four- or five-step rush." },

  // ---- PASS_PROTECTION ----
  "PROTECTION_PROTECTION_COUNT": { text: "Protection count is the number of blockers (offensive linemen plus any backs or tight ends kept in) assigned to block on a given pass play -- a \"6-man protection\" has the five linemen plus one extra blocker, while \"5-man\" or \"empty\" protection sends every eligible receiver out and blocks with the line alone." },
  "PROTECTION_EMPTY_PROTECTION": { text: "Empty protection blocks a pass play with only the offensive line (no running back or tight end kept in), sending every other eligible receiver into a route -- it maximizes receiving options but leaves the quarterback with the thinnest possible protection if the defense brings more rushers than blockers." },
  "PROTECTION_HALF_SLIDE": { text: "Half slide (or \"slide protection\") has the offensive line on one side of the center block gap-to-gap in one direction (the slide side), while the linemen on the other side and a running back use man-on-man rules -- a common way to protect against an overload blitz to the slide side while still covering the backside with a back." },
  "PROTECTION_FULL_SLIDE": { text: "Full slide has every offensive lineman shift their gap responsibility one direction across the entire line, with a running back or tight end responsible for anyone left unblocked on the backside -- simpler for the line to communicate than man protection, but it can leave the back on an island against a good backside rusher." },
  "PROTECTION_BOB_BIG_ON_BIG": { text: "BOB (big-on-big) protection assigns each offensive lineman a specific defender man-to-man based on down-lineman alignment, with backs picking up any remaining linebacker blitzers -- \"big-on-big\" means linemen always take the biggest, most immediate threats up front rather than sliding as a unit." },
  "PROTECTION_MAX_PROTECTION": { text: "Max protection keeps extra blockers in -- often both a running back and a tight end, in addition to the offensive line -- to protect a deep-developing pass play, at the cost of reducing the number of real receiving options for the quarterback." },
  "PROTECTION_HARD_RUN_ACTION": { text: "Hard run action is a play-action fake where the offensive line and running back sell an actual running play as convincingly as possible (full run-blocking steps, a real fake handoff) before the quarterback pulls the ball to pass -- the more the fake looks like a real run, the more it can freeze linebackers and safeties from dropping into coverage." },
  "PROTECTION_BOOT_PROTECTION": { text: "Boot(leg) protection accompanies a quarterback rolling away from where the offensive line is sliding or blocking, so the pocket the line builds is actually behind and to the side of where the quarterback ends up -- usually paired with one or two blockers releasing late to pick up backside pursuit." },
  "PROTECTION_VERTICAL_SET": { text: "A vertical set has a pass blocker retreat almost straight back off the snap, prioritizing depth to buy time against a speed rush around the edge -- the standard technique for a tackle protecting a quarterback who's dropping deep to throw." },
  "PROTECTION_45_DEGREE_SET": { text: "A 45-degree set angles a lineman's initial kick-slide diagonally rather than straight back, splitting the difference between cutting off an inside rush and an outside rush -- common for guards, who have to account for threats from either side more than a tackle does." },
  "PROTECTION_JUMP_SET": { text: "A jump set has an offensive lineman attack forward at the snap to meet a rusher at or near the line of scrimmage, rather than retreating into a normal drop-back set -- used against speed rushers to take away their runway, at the risk of getting beaten inside if the jump-setter overcommits outside." },
  "PROTECTION_POST_FOOT_INSIDE_HALF": { text: "Post-foot-inside-half is a protection rule telling a blocker to keep his inside foot planted and his body responsible for the inside half of his rushing lane first, passing off anyone who tries to cross his face to the inside rather than chasing them himself." },
  "PROTECTION_TWIST_EXCHANGE": { text: "A twist exchange is the pass-blocking side of a defensive stunt where two rushers cross paths -- the two blockers involved have to communicate and exchange rushers in real time (see \"passing off a twist\") rather than each simply staying on the man he started on." },
  "PROTECTION_HOT_ROUTE": { text: "A hot route is a receiver's built-in adjustment against a blitz the protection can't fully account for -- instead of running his called route, he breaks it off short and quick so the quarterback can get rid of the ball immediately against extra, unblocked rushers." },
  "PROTECTION_SIGHT_ADJUSTMENT": { text: "A sight adjustment is a receiver and quarterback silently agreeing, based on what the defense shows before the snap, to change a route without a verbal or signaled call -- both players simply \"see\" the same coverage picture and react to it the same way." },
  "PROTECTION_CHECK_RELEASE": { text: "Check release tells a running back or tight end to check (scan) for a blitzing defender first before releasing into his route -- if no one needs to be picked up, he releases as called; if someone does, blocking comes first and the route is abandoned." },
  "PROTECTION_GREEN_DOG": { text: "A green dog (or \"green dog blitz\") has a linebacker who's initially assigned to cover a running back rush the passer instead, but only if that running back stays in to block rather than releasing into a route -- since there's no receiver left to cover, the linebacker is freed to blitz." },
  "PROTECTION_SIMULATED_PRESSURE": { text: "Simulated pressure shows a pre-snap look with extra rushers threatening the line, then drops some of those same defenders into coverage at the snap while rushing only the normal number -- designed to confuse protection calls and quarterback pre-snap reads without actually blitzing more defenders than usual." },
  "PROTECTION_OVERLOAD": { text: "An overload blitz sends more rushers to one side of the formation than the offense has blockers to account for on that side, guaranteeing at least one free rusher unless the offense correctly slides its protection to match it before the snap." },
  "PROTECTION_PRESSURE_RESPONSIBILITY": { text: "Pressure responsibility is the specific rusher (or rushers) each individual pass protector is accountable for on a given protection call -- miscommunicating pressure responsibility, not a lack of talent, is the most common real cause of an unblocked free rusher." },

  // ---- ROUTE_TREE ----
  "ROUTE_HITCH_STOP": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_STICK", text: "A hitch (or stop) route has the receiver run straight upfield a short distance, then plant and turn back toward the quarterback, settling in open space in front of a defender playing off coverage -- the classic \"give me positive yards against a soft corner\" route." },
  "ROUTE_FLAT": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_SLANT_FLAT", text: "A flat route has the receiver (often a running back or tight end) run quickly and directly toward the sideline just a few yards downfield -- usually the shortest, quickest-developing route in a progression, meant to be open almost immediately against zone coverage's flat defender." },
  "ROUTE_SLANT": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_SLANT_FLAT", text: "A slant route breaks sharply inside at a 45-degree angle just a few steps off the line -- one of the fastest-developing routes in football, designed to beat both press man coverage (with a quick, decisive break) and soft zone (by getting the ball out before underneath defenders can close)." },
  "ROUTE_COMEBACK": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_SMASH", text: "A comeback route has the receiver push vertically upfield, then plant and come back toward the quarterback at an angle, typically breaking back outside toward the sideline -- timed for a quarterback throwing before the receiver even finishes his break, since the whole point is separation created by the defender's own forward momentum." },
  "ROUTE_CURL": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_HANK_CURL_FLAT", text: "A curl route pushes vertically upfield before turning back to face the quarterback (like a hitch, but breaking from a deeper depth) -- usually run to settle in the soft spot of a zone between an underneath defender and a deep defender." },
  "ROUTE_OUT": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_SMASH", text: "An out route breaks sharply toward the sideline at a fixed depth, at roughly a 90-degree angle from the receiver's stem -- a strong throw for a quarterback against zone coverage since it attacks the space between an underneath flat defender and a deep outside defender, but a dangerous sideline throw against man coverage without good depth and timing." },
  "ROUTE_DIG_SQUARE_IN": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_LEVELS", text: "A dig (or square-in) route breaks sharply toward the middle of the field at a fixed depth, at roughly a 90-degree angle -- one of the most effective routes against single-high, zone-based coverages because it crosses the field into space vacated by defenders occupied with deeper or shorter threats." },
  "ROUTE_CORNER_FLAG": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_SMASH", text: "A corner (or flag) route breaks at an angle toward the back corner of the end zone or sideline, roughly 45 degrees outside and upfield -- a common answer to Cover 2's deep-half safeties, since it's thrown into the specific window between the corner's underneath zone and the safety's deep half." },
  "ROUTE_POST": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_FOUR_VERTICALS", text: "A post route breaks at an angle toward the goalpost -- inside and upfield, roughly 45 degrees -- one of the classic ways to attack a single-high safety, since it aims directly at the seam that safety alone has to cover." },
  "ROUTE_GO_FADE_VERTICAL": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_FOUR_VERTICALS", text: "A go (or fade/vertical) route is the simplest route in football: run straight upfield as fast as possible, no break at all -- used to take the top off a defense, occupy a deep defender's full attention, or win a straight speed matchup against a cornerback." },
  "ROUTE_SEAM": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_FOUR_VERTICALS", text: "A seam route runs vertically up the \"seam\" between two zone defenders -- typically between a cornerback's deep-third and a safety's deep-half, or up the inside void in a two-high shell -- putting one specific defender in conflict between two vertical or horizontal threats." },
  "ROUTE_GLANCE": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_FOUR_VERTICALS", text: "A glance route is a shallow, quick-breaking post -- the receiver takes only a couple of steps before angling inside, rather than the deep post's longer stem -- designed to be thrown almost immediately, often as a hot route or built-in answer against pressure." },
  "ROUTE_SKINNY_POST": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_FOUR_VERTICALS", text: "A skinny post breaks at a much shallower angle toward the middle than a normal post -- closer to a seam that eventually bends inside -- designed to split two deep defenders in a two-high shell more precisely than a full 45-degree post would." },
  "ROUTE_DRAG_SHALLOW": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_MESH", text: "A drag (or shallow cross) route runs nearly parallel to the line of scrimmage at a shallow depth, all the way across the formation -- the signature route of Mesh concepts, designed to be open quickly against man coverage by running defenders into each other, or to sit down in a soft zone underneath." },
  "ROUTE_OVER_DEEP_CROSS": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_Y_CROSS", text: "An over (or deep cross) route runs a similar path to a shallow drag but at a much deeper depth, usually 12-18 yards downfield -- the signature route of Y-Cross concepts, meant to work across the field against man coverage's natural difficulty staying attached through traffic." },
  "ROUTE_WHEEL": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_FLOOD", text: "A wheel route starts like a flat or shallow route toward the sideline, then turns upfield and runs vertically along the sideline -- designed to catch a defender who thought the route was finished breaking short, especially effective out of the backfield against a linebacker in man coverage." },
  "ROUTE_ANGLE_TEXAS": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_DRIVE", text: "An angle (or Texas) route has a running back release toward the flat, then break sharply back inside -- the opposite shape of a wheel route -- designed to work against linebackers who overrun the initial flat release." },
  "ROUTE_OPTION_CHOICE": { text: "An option (or choice) route lets the receiver pick his own break -- in or out, deep or short -- based on how the defender covering him plays it, rather than running a pre-determined path. It requires the quarterback and receiver to read the same leverage the same way, since the quarterback has to throw to wherever the receiver decides to break." },
  "ROUTE_WHIP_PIVOT": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_STICK", text: "A whip (or pivot) route has the receiver break sharply one direction, then immediately reverse field and break back the other way -- designed to shake a man defender who commits to the first, fake break." },
  "ROUTE_RETURN_CHINA": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_SNAG", text: "A return (or China) route has the receiver run outside toward the sideline, then break back underneath toward the middle of the field -- often paired with a corner route from another receiver in a China/\"smash\"-style combination, occupying the flat defender before working back to open space." },
  "ROUTE_SIT_HOOK": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_STICK", text: "A sit (or hook) route pushes upfield and simply stops, turning to face the quarterback in a soft spot of the defense without any lateral break at all -- the most basic way to give a scrambling or pressured quarterback a reliable checkdown option." },
  "ROUTE_LEAK_DELAY": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_DRIVE", text: "A leak (or delay) route has an offensive lineman or tight end who initially shows pass-blocking or run-blocking, then releases late into a route once the defense has committed to thinking he's a blocker -- a delayed, deceptive release rather than an immediate one off the snap." },
  "ROUTE_FADE_BACK_SHOULDER": { diagramOverride: "PASSCONCEPT_FOUR_VERTICALS", text: "A back-shoulder fade is thrown behind a receiver running a normal go/fade route, timed for the receiver to stop his route and turn back to the ball the instant the defender's momentum carries him past the throw -- a route/throw combination that only works with precise, repped timing between quarterback and receiver." },
  "ROUTE_SCREEN_PATH": { text: "A screen path describes a receiver or running back's route on a screen pass -- releasing shallow and often laterally, timed with blockers releasing ahead of him, rather than running a normal downfield pattern. The route itself is secondary to the blocking scheme that develops in front of the ball carrier after the catch." },
  "ROUTE_ROUTE_STEM_DIAGNOSTIC": { text: "A route's stem is the initial straight-line path a receiver runs before making his break -- reading the stem (its depth, its speed, subtle head or shoulder cues) is one of the core skills a defensive back develops to diagnose a route before the break actually happens, rather than reacting to the break itself a half-second late." },

  // ---- FORMATIONS ----
  "FORMATION_2X2_BALANCED": { diagramOverride: "FORMATION_SHOTGUN", text: "A 2x2 balanced formation splits four eligible receivers evenly, two to each side of the formation -- the most common, symmetrical spread-passing look, giving the offense equal receiving strength both directions and forcing a defense to declare its coverage rotation without an obvious formation tell." },
  "FORMATION_TREY": { diagramOverride: "FORMATION_BUNCH", text: "Trey is a three-receiver formation on one side where the receivers are tightly grouped but staggered in a triangle rather than a true bunch -- similar goals to a Bunch look (natural picks, rub potential) with slightly more spacing between the three receivers." },
  "FORMATION_STACK": { diagramOverride: "FORMATION_BUNCH", text: "A stack formation lines up two receivers directly behind one another (rather than side-by-side) before the snap -- the trailing receiver is naturally shielded from a press defender by his teammate in front, making it a common way to guarantee a clean release against physical man coverage." },
  "FORMATION_QUADS_4X1": { diagramOverride: "FORMATION_EMPTY", text: "Quads (or 4x1) puts four eligible receivers to one side of the formation with only the quarterback (in shotgun) on the other, heavily overloading the defense's coverage to one side and often forcing a favorable one-on-one matchup on the backside single receiver." },
  "FORMATION_3X2_EMPTY": { diagramOverride: "FORMATION_EMPTY", text: "3x2 empty splits five eligible receivers three-to-one-side, two-to-the-other with no running back in the backfield at all -- a common base empty-set shape that still gives the offense a real numbers advantage or matchup answer to almost any coverage look." },
  "FORMATION_NUB": { text: "A nub formation has a tight end attached to the offensive line with no receiver split out to that same side at all -- leaving that side of the formation deliberately short on receivers, which often signals a run play toward the nub side (extra blocker, no receiver to threaten downfield) unless the offense has built in play-action off that same look." },
  "FORMATION_WING_H_BACK_Y_OFF": { diagramOverride: "FORMATION_I_FORMATION", text: "A wing, H-back, or Y-off alignment puts an extra blocker/receiver hybrid just off the formation's edge -- behind the line and outside the tackle -- giving the offense a versatile piece who can block like a tight end, release like a receiver, or go in motion, all from the same static pre-snap look." },
  "FORMATION_CONDENSED_NASTY_SPLITS": { text: "Condensed (or \"nasty\") splits have the receivers line up much tighter to the offensive line than a normal wide alignment -- often just a yard or two outside the tackle -- usually to help with run blocking (a receiver close enough to crack back on a linebacker) or to create natural rub/pick opportunities against tight man coverage." },
  "FORMATION_UNBALANCED_LINE": { text: "An unbalanced line shifts an offensive lineman (often a tackle) to the opposite side of the formation, giving one side of the line an extra blocker beyond the normal five-man symmetry -- a rare, deliberately deceptive alignment used to gain a numbers advantage in a specific run direction." },
  "FORMATION_WILDCAT_DIRECT_SNAP": { text: "Wildcat (or direct-snap) formation snaps the ball directly to a running back or receiver lined up in the shotgun/pistol spot instead of the quarterback, turning that player into an immediate running or option threat the instant he catches the snap, since there's no mesh point or handoff to slow the play down." },
  "FORMATION_DIAMOND_PISTOL": { diagramOverride: "FORMATION_PISTOL", text: "Diamond pistol adds two extra backs flanking the running back in a pistol alignment, forming a diamond shape with the quarterback -- giving the offense three backfield blockers/ball carriers behind the quarterback for heavy run packages or goal-line situations, while keeping the pistol's straight-on quarterback depth." },

  // ---- DEFENSIVE_FRONTS ----
  "FRONT_TITE_FRONT": { diagramOverride: "FRONT_ODD_FRONT", text: "A tite front (or \"tite-4\") lines up defensive linemen in the 4i technique (inside shoulder of both tackles) plus a nose over the center, taking away inside gaps entirely and daring the offense to run outside or throw underneath -- a modern answer to spread run-game schemes built around gap-scheme interior runs." },
  "FRONT_MINT_FRONT": { diagramOverride: "FRONT_ODD_FRONT", text: "A mint front is a variation of the tite/odd front family that shifts the defensive tackles slightly to create a different gap-control picture up front while keeping the same core \"take away the interior, funnel runs outside\" philosophy." },
  "FRONT_WIDE_9": { diagramOverride: "FRONT_4_3_OVER", text: "Wide-9 aligns the defensive ends far outside the tackle box, well outside their normal position, to maximize their speed rush angle to the quarterback -- a pass-rush-first front that sacrifices some run-gap integrity on the edge for a faster path around the tackle." },
  "FRONT_5_2_6_1_GOAL_LINE": { diagramOverride: "FRONT_BEAR_FRONT", text: "A 5-2 (or 6-1) goal-line front packs extra defensive linemen and linebackers directly on the line of scrimmage, covering nearly every gap with a lineman rather than a linebacker -- used almost exclusively in short-yardage and goal-line situations where stopping a one- or two-yard gain matters more than pass-rush speed or coverage numbers." },
  "FRONT_ODD": { diagramOverride: "FRONT_ODD_FRONT", text: "An odd front, broadly, describes any defensive line alignment with a nose tackle directly over the center (an \"odd\" number of linemen on the line relative to the center) -- the family that includes the 3-4, Odd Front, Tite, and Mint fronts, all built around controlling both A-gaps with one defender." },
  "FRONTTECH_SLANT": { text: "A slant has the entire defensive line move laterally and diagonally at the snap, all stepping to the same gap in the same direction rather than staying in their pre-snap alignment -- designed to beat down blocks and gap-scheme runs by having the defender already moving into the gap the blocker is trying to reach." },
  "FRONTTECH_STUNT": { text: "A stunt has two or more defensive linemen (or a lineman and a linebacker) cross paths and exchange rushing lanes at the snap -- one loops around while the other shoots the gap the looping player vacated -- designed to confuse pass-protection assignments and create free access to the quarterback." },
  "TECH_GET_OFF": { text: "Get-off is a defensive lineman's initial quickness out of his stance at the snap -- how fast he crosses the line of scrimmage relative to the snap count -- often the single biggest separator between an average and elite pass rusher, since a great get-off beats a blocker's set before he's even fully out of his stance." },
  "TECH_PAD_LEVEL_LEVERAGE": { text: "Pad level is how low a defender's shoulders are relative to the blocker in front of him at the moment of contact -- the lower man almost always wins the leverage battle, since he can get underneath the blocker's pads and drive him backward rather than absorbing the blocker's own leverage." },
  "TECH_STRIKE_AND_SEPARATION": { text: "Strike and separation is a pass rusher's ability to land an initial hand strike on the blocker, then immediately create space between himself and the blocker rather than staying locked up -- winning the strike alone isn't enough if the rusher can't disengage afterward to actually reach the quarterback." },
  "TECH_TWO_GAP_CONTROL": { text: "Two-gap control asks a defensive lineman to control the blocker in front of him first, reading where the ball goes, then shed the blocker into whichever of his two assigned gaps the ball carrier actually enters -- a read-and-react technique, as opposed to attacking one gap immediately." },
  "TECH_ONE_GAP_ATTACK": { text: "One-gap attack asks a defensive lineman to fire immediately into one single assigned gap at the snap, penetrating upfield rather than reading the blocker first -- trading some risk of getting out of position for a faster, more disruptive get-upfield." },
  "TECH_WRONG_ARM_TECHNIQUE": { text: "Wrong-arm technique has a defender deliberately attack a blocker with the arm and shoulder closer to the ball carrier, spinning the blocker's body away from the play rather than holding the gap he's aligned in -- a way to squeeze an outside run back inside into pursuit, at the cost of giving up the gap he was originally responsible for." },
  "TECH_BOX_TECHNIQUE": { text: "Box technique describes a defender playing square, controlled, two-gapping football with both hands, staying square to the line of scrimmage and reacting rather than immediately attacking one direction -- common for bigger interior linemen whose job is to occupy blockers rather than make plays themselves." },
  "TECH_LONG_ARM_RUSH": { text: "A long-arm rush extends a pass rusher's arm fully into a blocker's chest to create separation and stop his own forward progress from being stalled, using length and leverage rather than a hand-fighting or speed move to beat the block." },
  "TECH_SPEED_TO_POWER": { text: "Speed-to-power is a pass-rush move where a defender starts with an outside speed rush, forcing the blocker to turn and open his hips wide to the edge, then converts that speed into a bull-rush push straight through the blocker's now-compromised base." },
  "TECH_CROSS_CHOP": { text: "A cross-chop combines a two-handed swipe across a blocker's arms (knocking them out of the way) with a simultaneous chop or club move, meant to disrupt a blocker's hand placement entirely before he can ever lock on." },
  "TECH_INSIDE_COUNTER": { text: "An inside counter is a pass-rush move where a defender sells an outside speed rush, then immediately cuts back inside once the blocker over-commits to protecting the edge -- the pass-rush equivalent of a running back's jump-cut off a defender's own momentum." },
  "TECH_RUSH_LANE_INTEGRITY": { text: "Rush-lane integrity means every pass rusher stays in his own assigned rushing lane rather than crashing toward the ball, so the quarterback has nowhere to step up or scramble through -- a discipline point especially important on plays designed to bait rushers upfield before a quarterback escapes underneath them." },
  "TECH_DOUBLE_TEAM_DISPLACEMENT": { text: "Double-team displacement measures how far back (or how little) two blockers together can move a single defender off the line of scrimmage -- a defender who can hold his ground or even split a double-team is one of the hardest matchups in football to game-plan around." },
  "TECH_LINE_GAMES": { text: "Line games are pre-planned stunts and twists built into a defensive front's call, designed to create confusion and free rushers against a specific protection scheme -- the defensive line's equivalent of an offense's route combination, with its own built-in answers to how the offense is expected to protect." },
  "TECH_SURFACE_ARITHMETIC": { text: "Surface arithmetic is the numbers math a defense does pre-snap along the offensive formation's \"surface\" (from the ball to the widest player) -- comparing blockers to rushers on that surface to decide whether the front has enough numbers to stop a run to that side without needing help from the secondary." }
};
